      109  Taoism and Confucianism
      110  [ edit ]
      111  Main articles: Taoism and Confucianism
      112  Taoism in Taiwan is almost entirely entwined with folk religion , [ 40 ] as it is mostly of the Zhengyi school in which priests function as ritual ministers of local communities' cults. [ 40 ] Taiwanese Taoism lacks a contemplative , ascetic and monastic tradition such as northern China's Quanzhen Taoism . The Celestial Masters , leaders of the Zhengyi school, have their seat on the island. Nowada
      113  Politicians of all parties appear at Taoist temples during campaigns, using them for political gatherings. [ 40 ] Despite this and the contention among sects for leadership, there is no unitary structure of authority overseeing all Taoists in Taiwan. [ 40 ] According to the 2005 census, there were 7.6 million Taoists in Taiwan (33% of the population) in that year. [ 41 ] As of 2015, there were 9,4
      114  Confucianism is present in Taiwan in the form of many associations and temples and shrines for the worship of Confucius and sages . [ 43 ] In 2005, 0.7% of the population of Taiwan adhered to Xuanyuanism , which is a Confucian-based religion worshipping Huangdi as the symbol of God. [ 44 ]
      115  Temple of the Great Peace in Caotun, Nantou.
      116  Sanye Temple in Bao-an, Tainan.
      117  Ceremony at a Temple of Confucius in Chiayi.
      118  Buddhism
      119  [ edit ]
      120  Main article: Buddhism in Taiwan
      121  Buddhism was introduced into Taiwan in the mid- Qing dynasty (18th century) through the zhaijiao popular sects. [ 11 ] Several forms of Buddhism thrived in Taiwan ever since. During the Japanese occupation, Japanese schools of Buddhism (such as Shingon Buddhism , Jōdo Shinshū , Nichiren Shū ) gained influence over many Taiwanese Buddhist temples as part of the Japanese policy of cultural assimilat
      122  Although many Buddhist communities affiliated with Japanese sects for protection, they largely retained Chinese Buddhist practices. For instance, the Japanisation of Chinese Buddhism, the introduction of clerical marriage and the practice of eating meat and drinking wine, was not as successful as in the Buddhist tradition of Japanese-occupied Korea . [ 17 ]
      123  Following the end of World War II and the establishment of the Republic of China on the island, many monks from mainland China moved to Taiwan, including Yin Shun (印順) who is generally considered to be the key figure who brought Humanistic Buddhism to Taiwan. They contributed significantly to the development of Chinese Buddhism on the island.
      124  The Buddhist Association of the Republic of China remained the dominant Buddhist organization until the end of restrictions on religious activities in the 1980s. Today there are several large Humanistic Buddhist and Buddhist modernist organisations in Taiwan, including the Dharma Drum Mountain ( Fǎgǔshān 法鼓山 ) founded by Sheng Yen ( 聖嚴 ), Buddha's Light International ( Fóguāngshān 佛光山 ) founded by
      125  The zhaijiao Buddhist groups maintain an influence in society. In recent decades, also non-Chinese forms of Buddhism, such as Tibetan Buddhism and Soka Gakkai Nichiren Buddhism , have expanded in Taiwan. [ 33 ] Adherence to Buddhism has grown significantly in Taiwan since the 1980s. [ 32 ] From 800.000 in 1983 (4% of the population), the number of Buddhists expanded to 4.9 million in 1995 and subs
      126  Shuang Ho Jing Temple of the Tzu Chi , in Zhonghe, New Taipei.
