      131  Chinese salvationism
      132  [ edit ]
      133  Main article: Chinese salvationist religions
      134  As of 2005, more than 10% of the population of Taiwan adhered to a variety of folk religious organisations of salvation. The largest of them is Yiguandao (with 3.5% of the population), followed by Tiandiism (whose two churches, the Holy Church of the Heavenly Virtue and the Lord of Universe Church, counted together constitute 2.2% of the island's population), Miledadao (an offshoot of Yiguandao ac
      135  A Yiguandao-organised Confucian ceremony for the worship of gods and Heaven in March 2017. Yiguandao elders are those clad in grey robes.
      136  The Luanist Rebirth Church (重生堂 Chóngshēngtáng ) in Taichung.
      137  Minor religions
      138  [ edit ]
      139  Christianity
      140  [ edit ]
      141  Main article: Christianity in Taiwan
      142  Further information: Catholic Church in Taiwan and Eastern Orthodoxy in Taiwan
      143  Christianity in Taiwan constituted 3.9% of the population, according to the census of 2005; [ 41 ] Christians in Taiwan included approximately 600,000 Protestants , 300,000 Catholics and a small number of members of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints .
      144  Estimates in 2020 suggested that the portion had risen to 4% or 6%. [ 46 ] [ 47 ]
      145  Despite its minority status, many of the early Kuomintang political leaders of the Republic of China were Christians. Several Republic of China presidents have been Christians, including Sun Yat-sen who was a Congregationalist , Chiang Kai-shek and Chiang Ching-kuo who were Methodists , Lee Teng-hui is a member of the Presbyterian Church . The Presbyterian Church has strong ties with the Democrati
      146  Christianity in Taiwan has declined since the 1970s, after strong growth from 1950 to the 1960s. [ 48 ]
