      153  Islam
      154  [ edit ]
      155  Main article: Islam in Taiwan
      156  Taipei Grand Mosque in Daan, Taipei.
      157  Though Islam originated in the Arabian Peninsula , it had spread eastward to China as early as the 7th century CE. Muslim merchants married local Chinese women, creating a new Chinese ethnic group called the Hui people . Islam first reached Taiwan in the 17th century when Muslim families from the southern China's coastal province of Fujian accompanied Koxinga on his invasion to oust the Dutch from
      158  During the Chinese Civil War , some 20,000 Muslims, mostly soldiers and civil servants, fled mainland China with the Kuomintang to Taiwan. Since the 1980s, thousands of Muslims from Myanmar and Thailand, who are descendants of nationalist soldiers who fled Yunnan as a result of the communist takeover, have migrated to Taiwan in search of a better life. In more recent years, there has been a rise i
      159  Baháʼí Faith
      160  [ edit ]
      161  Main article: Baháʼí Faith in Taiwan
      162  The history of the Baháʼí Faith ( Chinese : 巴哈伊教 ; pinyin : Bāhāyījiào ) in Taiwan began after the religion entered areas of China [ 49 ] and nearby Japan. [ 50 ] The first Baháʼís arrived in Taiwan in 1949 [ 51 ] and the first of these to have become a Baháʼí was Jerome Chu (Chu Yao-lung) in 1945 while visiting the United States. By May 1955 there were eighteen Baháʼís in six localities across Ta
      163  Judaism
      164  [ edit ]
      165  Main article: History of the Jews in Taiwan
      166  There has been a Jewish community in Taiwan since the 1950s. [ 52 ] Since 2011, there has been a Chabad in Taipei. [ 53 ]
